Q: Linear Search algorithm code in java Programming:

What is a linear search:
ans:Linear search is the simplest way to find an element in a list or array.

It works by checking each element one by one from the beginning until:

the target element is found, or
the list ends (meaning the element isn’t present)

How it works:
1.Start from the first element
2.Compare it with the target value
3.If it matches → return its position
4.If not → move to the next element
5.Repeat until found or list ends


package linearsearch;

import java.util.*;

public class LinearSearch {
     public static void main(String[] args) {
         //variable initialization
         int n,i,key,c=0;
         //input array
         int[] a;
         Scanner sc=new Scanner(System.in);
         System.out.println("How many element do you want to enter");
         n=sc.nextInt();
         a=new int[n];
         
         System.out.println("Enter Elements");
         
         //loop for array
         for(i=0; i<n; i++){
             a[i]=sc.nextInt();
         }
         System.out.println("Enter item to find:");
         key=sc.nextInt();
         
         //loop to itrate or check key 
         for(i=0; i<a.length; i++){
             if(a[i]==key)
             {
                 c++;
                 break;
             }
         }
         
         //to check key match with array element or not 
        if(c>0){
            
            System.out.println("item exist at index="+i);
             }
        else{
            System.out.println("item does not exist" );
             }
         
         
     }
}

